{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q7Detzitusz1M2FS2vop4s","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Exact FRNN performance report\n2\t\n3\t## Scope and revisions\n4\t\n5\t- Baseline implementation: `a35c71322750`\n6\t- Measured optimized implementation: `2a5da2907916`\n7\t- Branch: `improve`\n8\t\n9\tThe optimized implementation preserves the standalone C++17/CUDA API and has\n10\tno production dependency on Torch, ATen, c10, Python, or TBB. Python remains\n11\tan optional NumPy binding over the same C++ library.\n12\t\n13\t## Measurement environment\n14\t\n15\t- GPU: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2070 SUPER, compute capability 7.5, 8 GiB\n16\t- Driver: 580.173.02\n17\t- CUDA Toolkit/runtime: 12.0.140\n18\t- CMake: 3.28.3\n19\t- C++ compiler: GNU 13.3.0\n20\t- CPU: AMD Ryzen 5 3600 6-Core Processor\n21\t- Build: Release\n22\t- CMake CUDA architecture: 52 (the environment's detected/default value)\n23\t- Synthetic warmup/measured iterations: 10/50\n24\t- Real warmup/measured iterations: 10/30\n25\t\n26\tCUDA-event timings record on the call's stream and synchronize the stop event.\n27\tHost timings use `std::chrono::steady_clock` and synchronize before stopping.\n28\tThe raw machine-readable results are:\n29\t\n30\t- `benchmarks/results/baseline_a35c713.csv`\n31\t- `benchmarks/results/final_2a5da29.csv`\n32\t- `benchmarks/results/final_real_2a5da29.csv`\n33\t\n34\t## Final architecture\n35\t\n36\tAutomatic dispatch uses exact CUDA brute force when the overflow-safe\n37\tcoordinate/selection work estimate is at most 2,000,000. All larger workloads\n38\tuse the uniform grid. Both paths evaluate every dimension, use an inclusive\n39\tradius, and retain candidates by `(squared_distance, original_index)`.\n40\t\n41\tThe grid pipeline:\n42\t\n43\t1. computes database bounds;\n44\t2. chooses radius-sized cells for up to three grid dimensions and radius/2\n45\t   cells for four grid dimensions;\n46\t3. caps dense metadata at 131,072 cells for dimensions 1–4 and 2,097,152 cells\n47\t   for dimensions 5–32;\n48\t4. assigns cells, scans counts, and counting-sorts points;\n49\t5. processes identical-set queries in spatial order while restoring original\n50\t   output rows;\n51\t6. evaluates specialized dimensions 1/2/3/4/8/12/16 or the generic path;\n52\t7. exits distance accumulation as soon as its partial sum exceeds the radius;\n53\t8. uses sorted insertion for short rows and converts to a deterministic\n54\t   max-heap after 24 candidates when K is at least 64;\n55\t9. counts, scans, and writes final edges.\n56\t\n57\tInternal neighbor rows use 32-bit indices and one lazy `-1` sentinel. The\n58\tpublic edge type remains signed 64-bit. The device API stays fully\n59\tasynchronous and supports both full-output and count-only modes. The host API\n60\tuses count-only search, allocates exact edge storage, then writes without\n61\trerunning search.\n62\t\n63\t## Correctness\n64\t\n65\tThe independent CPU oracle in `tests/test_frnn.cpp` computes all-pairs\n66\t`float32` squared distance, inclusive radius membership, deterministic\n67\tdistance/index sorting, K truncation, and edge post-processing.\n68\t\n69\tThe core test runs 2,500 randomized cases through forced grid, forced CUDA\n70\tbrute force, and automatic dispatch: 7,500 path comparisons per run. Coverage\n71\tincludes dimensions 1/2/3/4/8/16/32; K 0/1/4/8/16/32/64; empty and dense\n72\tneighborhoods; identical and separate sets; duplicate, clustered, correlated,\n73\tsorted, anisotropic, and degenerate coordinates; exact and adjacent\n74\t`nextafter` radius boundaries; ties; reused workspaces; alternating sizes;\n75\tcount-only calls; and default/non-default streams.\n76\t\n77\tThe real reference validation is exact:\n78\t\n79\t| Item | Result |\n80\t|---|---:|\n81\t| Embedding | 271,663 points × 12 dimensions |\n82\t| Radius / K | 0.12 / 1000 |\n83\t| Reference directed edges | 9,279,672 |\n84\t| Output directed edges | 9,279,672 |\n85\t| Missing / extra | 0 / 0 |\n86\t| Precision / recall | 1.0 / 1.0 |\n87\t| Repeated host/Python time | 0.675 s |\n88\t\n89\tThe CUDA core, Python binding suite, and installed CMake consumer all form\n90\tseparate validation layers. Compute Sanitizer could not run because this\n91\tmachine's installation lacks `libsanitizer-collection.so`; Nsight limitations\n92\tare detailed in `docs/optimization_log.md`.\n93\t\n94\t## Performance\n95\t\n96\tWarm device latency:\n97\t\n98\t| Workload | Baseline p50 / p95 | Final p50 / p95 | p50 speedup |\n99\t|---|---:|---:|---:|\n100\t| 128, D=3, K=4, sparse | 0.168 / 0.170 ms | 0.058 / 0.059 ms | 2.88x |\n101\t| 512, D=3, K=32, dense | 1.031 / 1.037 ms | 0.776 / 0.792 ms | 1.33x |\n102\t| 4,096, D=3, K=16, sparse | 0.454 / 0.459 ms | 0.235 / 0.240 ms | 1.93x |\n103\t| 10,000, D=8, K=16, sparse | 8.240 / 8.252 ms | 3.228 / 3.406 ms | 2.55x |\n104\t| 10,000 separate, D=4, K=32 | 2.733 / 2.754 ms | 2.751 / 2.769 ms | 0.99x |\n105\t\n106\tThe p50 geometric-mean speedup over these five immutable-baseline cases is\n107\t1.80x. The only primary-case regression is 0.6%, below the 10% acceptance\n108\tlimit.\n109\t\n110\tHost convenience API latency:\n111\t\n112\t| Workload | Baseline p50 | Final p50 | Speedup |\n113\t|---|---:|---:|---:|\n114\t| 128, D=3, K=4 | 0.706 ms | 0.119 ms | 5.94x |\n115\t| 512, D=3, K=32 | 1.604 ms | 1.027 ms | 1.56x |\n116\t| 4,096, D=3, K=16 | 1.372 ms | 0.552 ms | 2.49x |\n117\t| 10,000, D=8, K=16 | 9.520 ms | 4.246 ms | 2.24x |\n118\t| 10,000 separate, D=4, K=32 | 5.109 ms | 4.170 ms | 1.23x |\n119\t\n120\tReal device breakdown:\n121\t\n122\t| Phase | p50 | p95 |\n123\t|---|---:|---:|\n124\t| One-time workspace reserve | 45.245 ms | 46.958 ms |\n125\t| Cold device call | 401.182 ms | 401.985 ms |\n126\t| Warm device GPU | 356.277 ms | 358.379 ms |\n127\t| Warm synchronized host | 356.552 ms | 358.439 ms |\n128\t| 9.28M-edge device-to-host copy | 7.169 ms | 7.253 ms |\n129\t\n130\tThe brute-force crossover measurements support the dispatch rule:\n131\t\n132\t- 128/D=16/K=1: brute force 0.089 ms, grid 0.185 ms;\n133\t- 128/D=3/K=4: brute force 0.055 ms, grid 0.167 ms;\n134\t- 512/D=3/K=32 dense: grid 0.835 ms, brute force 1.121 ms;\n135\t- 1,000/D=4/K=8 sparse: grid 0.217 ms, brute force 0.277 ms at the time of\n136\t  the dispatch experiment.\n137\t\n138\tThe later 3D-grid/cell-cap optimization reduces the automatic 1,000/D=4/K=8\n139\tcase further to 0.125 ms.\n140\t\n141\t## Memory, compilation, and regressions\n142\t\n143\tFor the real host call, major device allocations are approximately:\n144\t\n145\t| Storage | Size |\n146\t|---|---:|\n147\t| Neighbor distance/index workspace | 2,173,304,000 bytes |\n148\t| Exact edge output | 148,474,752 bytes |\n149\t| Input and sorted coordinates | 26,079,648 bytes |\n150\t| Grid counts/offsets | 16,777,216 bytes |\n151\t| Point cell metadata | 2,173,304 bytes |\n152\t| Edge count/offset metadata | 4,346,624 bytes |\n153\t| Approximate total before scan scratch | 2,261 MiB |\n154\t\n155\tCount-first host allocation avoids the 4,146 MiB conservative edge output\n156\tthat would otherwise be required for N×K capacity.\n157\t\n158\tThe Release shared library grows from 168 KiB to 399 KiB (+231 KiB, 2.38x).\n159\tA clean parallel build on this machine grows from 4.01 seconds to 8.40 seconds\n160\t(2.09x), mainly from dimension-specialized kernels and the exact heap path.\n161\t\n162\tNo immutable primary benchmark regresses by 10%. Relative to the first broad\n163\tpost-optimization matrix, the D=8 duplicate distribution rises from 2.097 ms\n164\tto 2.364 ms (+12.7%) while the rest of that matrix generally improves. This\n165\tedge-case regression is retained because the final structural changes produce\n166\tlarge gains on empty, boundary, clustered, dense-K, large D=8, and 500k-point\n167\tcases, and the duplicate case remains exact.\n168\t\n169\t## Retained and rejected experiments\n170\t\n171\tRetained:\n172\t\n173\t- exact CUDA brute-force fallback and documented dispatch;\n174\t- early distance exit and common-dimension specializations;\n175\t- spatially ordered identical-set queries;\n176\t- lazy neighbor sentinel and 32-bit internal indices;\n177\t- dimension-sensitive 3D/4D grids and metadata ceilings;\n178\t- exact count-first host output allocation;\n179\t- density-triggered hybrid top-K heap.\n180\t\n181\tRejected or narrowed:\n182\t\n183\t- unconditional brute force for 512/D=3/K=32 and 1,000/D=4/K=8;\n184\t- a universal 131,072-cell grid cap, which made the real D=12 case 2.25x\n185\t  slower;\n186\t- radius-sized cells for four grid dimensions, which regressed large D=8;\n187\t- an unconditional K=64 heap, which doubled a sparse 50k-point case;\n188\t- a fourth grid dimension for D=4, which regressed the small D=4 case;\n189\t- thread-local K arrays, avoided after resource inspection showed existing\n190\t  D=8/D=16 stack and register pressure.\n191\t\n192\t## Remaining bottlenecks\n193\t\n194\t- High-dimensional search still indexes only four coordinates and can visit\n195\t  many false candidates for correlated or anisotropic data.\n196\t- Dense grid scans operate on a dimensionality-selected ceiling rather than a\n197\t  compact occupied-cell structure.\n198\t- Per-query selection is serial even after switching to a heap; a\n199\t  warp-cooperative design may help dense cells.\n200\t- Edge count, scan, write, and count copy remain separate stages.\n201\t- The synchronous host API still allocates a fresh workspace per call.\n202\t- CUDA Graph capture and a sorted-key/occupied-cell redesign remain credible\n203\t  larger projects, but neither had enough measured evidence to retain here.\n204\t\n205\t## Reproduction\n206\t\n207\t```bash\n208\tcmake -S . -B build \\\n209\t  -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \\\n210\t  -DFRNN_BUILD_TESTS=ON \\\n211\t  -DFRNN_BUILD_BENCHMARKS=ON\n212\tcmake --build build --parallel\n213\tctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure\n214\t\n215\t./build/frnn_benchmark \\\n216\t  --full --warmup 10 --iterations 50 \\\n217\t  --output benchmarks/results/reproduction.csv\n218\t\n219\t./build/frnn_benchmark \\\n220\t  --embedding data/embedding_data.csv \\\n221\t  --warmup 10 --iterations 30 \\\n222\t  --output benchmarks/results/reproduction-real.csv\n223\t\n224\tpython tests/compare_reference_edges.py --minimum-agreement 1.0\n225\t```\n226\t\n227\tFor the optional Python validation:\n228\t\n229\t```bash\n230\tpython -m pip install .\n231\tpython tests/test_python.py\n232\t```\n233\t\n234\tFor installed-library consumer validation:\n235\t\n236\t```bash\n237\tcmake --install build --prefix \"$PWD/install\"\n238\tcmake -S tests/consumer -B build-consumer \\\n239\t  -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=\"$PWD/install\"\n240\tcmake --build build-consumer --parallel\n241\t```\n242\t\n243\t## Final validation results\n244\t\n245\tThe required Release configuration, build, and CTest command completed\n246\tsuccessfully. The shared library installed, configured in\n247\t`tests/consumer`, built, and ran with output `1 0`. A separate\n248\t`BUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF` Release build passed CTest, installed, and its static\n249\tconsumer also built and ran with output `1 0`.\n250\t\n251\t`ldd build/libfrnn.so` reports CUDA runtime and standard system libraries\n252\tonly. Source/dependency inspection confirms no production Torch, ATen, c10,\n253\tPython, or TBB linkage. The only Python discovery in CMake is under the\n254\tdisabled-by-default `FRNN_BUILD_PYTHON` option.\n255\t\n256\t## Suggested pull request\n257\t\n258\tTitle:\n259\t\n260\t```text\n261\tOptimize exact standalone FRNN search and edge construction\n262\t```\n263\t\n264\tDescription:\n265\t\n266\t```text\n267\tAdds an exact CUDA brute-force fallback, dimension-sensitive 3D/4D grids,\n268\tspecialized early-exit distance kernels, spatially ordered same-set queries,\n269\tlazy compact neighbor rows, a density-triggered exact top-K heap, and\n270\tcount-first host output allocation. Expands differential testing to all\n271\tdispatch paths and adds reproducible synthetic/real benchmarks. The supplied\n272\t271,663×12 dataset matches all 9,279,672 reference edges exactly. Representative\n273\twarm GPU p50 improves by 1.80x geometric mean, including 2.88x on small sparse\n274\tand 2.55x on medium D=8 workloads.\n275\t```\n276\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f82439e-67c9-4df8-8006-0f75deb44a8f","uuid":"86c9240d-2932-44f6-a001-3ce5d5d7215a","timestamp":"2026-09-20T23:12:10.820Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/libFRNN/docs/performance_report.md","content":"# Exact FRNN performance report\n\n## Scope and revisions\n\n- Baseline implementation: `a35c71322750`\n- Measured optimized implementation: `2a5da2907916`\n- Branch: `improve`\n\nThe optimized implementation preserves the standalone C++17/CUDA API and has\nno production dependency on Torch, ATen, c10, Python, or TBB. Python remains\nan optional NumPy binding over the same C++ library.\n\n## Measurement environment\n\n- GPU: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2070 SUPER, compute capability 7.5, 8 GiB\n- Driver: 580.173.02\n- CUDA Toolkit/runtime: 12.0.140\n- CMake: 3.28.3\n- C++ compiler: GNU 13.3.0\n- CPU: AMD Ryzen 5 3600 6-Core Processor\n- Build: Release\n- CMake CUDA architecture: 52 (the environment's detected/default value)\n- Synthetic warmup/measured iterations: 10/50\n- Real warmup/measured iterations: 10/30\n\nCUDA-event timings record on the call's stream and synchronize the stop event.\nHost timings use `std::chrono::steady_clock` and synchronize before stopping.\nThe raw machine-readable results are:\n\n- `benchmarks/results/baseline_a35c713.csv`\n- `benchmarks/results/final_2a5da29.csv`\n- `benchmarks/results/final_real_2a5da29.csv`\n\n## Final architecture\n\nAutomatic dispatch uses exact CUDA brute force when the overflow-safe\ncoordinate/selection work estimate is at most 2,000,000. All larger workloads\nuse the uniform grid. Both paths evaluate every dimension, use an inclusive\nradius, and retain candidates by `(squared_distance, original_index)`.\n\nThe grid pipeline:\n\n1. computes database bounds;\n2. chooses radius-sized cells for up to three grid dimensions and radius/2\n   cells for four grid dimensions;\n3. caps dense metadata at 131,072 cells for dimensions 1–4 and 2,097,152 cells\n   for dimensions 5–32;\n4. assigns cells, scans counts, and counting-sorts points;\n5. processes identical-set queries in spatial order while restoring original\n   output rows;\n6. evaluates specialized dimensions 1/2/3/4/8/12/16 or the generic path;\n7. exits distance accumulation as soon as its partial sum exceeds the radius;\n8. uses sorted insertion for short rows and converts to a deterministic\n   max-heap after 24 candidates when K is at least 64;\n9. counts, scans, and writes final edges.\n\nInternal neighbor rows use 32-bit indices and one lazy `-1` sentinel. The\npublic edge type remains signed 64-bit. The device API stays fully\nasynchronous and supports both full-output and count-only modes. The host API\nuses count-only search, allocates exact edge storage, then writes without\nrerunning search.\n\n## Correctness\n\nThe independent CPU oracle in `tests/test_frnn.cpp` computes all-pairs\n`float32` squared distance, inclusive radius membership, deterministic\ndistance/index sorting, K truncation, and edge post-processing.\n\nThe core test runs 2,500 randomized cases through forced grid, forced CUDA\nbrute force, and automatic dispatch: 7,500 path comparisons per run. Coverage\nincludes dimensions 1/2/3/4/8/16/32; K 0/1/4/8/16/32/64; empty and dense\nneighborhoods; identical and separate sets; duplicate, clustered, correlated,\nsorted, anisotropic, and degenerate coordinates; exact and adjacent\n`nextafter` radius boundaries; ties; reused workspaces; alternating sizes;\ncount-only calls; and default/non-default streams.\n\nThe real reference validation is exact:\n\n| Item | Result |\n|---|---:|\n| Embedding | 271,663 points × 12 dimensions |\n| Radius / K | 0.12 / 1000 |\n| Reference directed edges | 9,279,672 |\n| Output directed edges | 9,279,672 |\n| Missing / extra | 0 / 0 |\n| Precision / recall | 1.0 / 1.0 |\n| Repeated host/Python time | 0.675 s |\n\nThe CUDA core, Python binding suite, and installed CMake consumer all form\nseparate validation layers. Compute Sanitizer could not run because this\nmachine's installation lacks `libsanitizer-collection.so`; Nsight limitations\nare detailed in `docs/optimization_log.md`.\n\n## Performance\n\nWarm device latency:\n\n| Workload | Baseline p50 / p95 | Final p50 / p95 | p50 speedup |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 128, D=3, K=4, sparse | 0.168 / 0.170 ms | 0.058 / 0.059 ms | 2.88x |\n| 512, D=3, K=32, dense | 1.031 / 1.037 ms | 0.776 / 0.792 ms | 1.33x |\n| 4,096, D=3, K=16, sparse | 0.454 / 0.459 ms | 0.235 / 0.240 ms | 1.93x |\n| 10,000, D=8, K=16, sparse | 8.240 / 8.252 ms | 3.228 / 3.406 ms | 2.55x |\n| 10,000 separate, D=4, K=32 | 2.733 / 2.754 ms | 2.751 / 2.769 ms | 0.99x |\n\nThe p50 geometric-mean speedup over these five immutable-baseline cases is\n1.80x. The only primary-case regression is 0.6%, below the 10% acceptance\nlimit.\n\nHost convenience API latency:\n\n| Workload | Baseline p50 | Final p50 | Speedup |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 128, D=3, K=4 | 0.706 ms | 0.119 ms | 5.94x |\n| 512, D=3, K=32 | 1.604 ms | 1.027 ms | 1.56x |\n| 4,096, D=3, K=16 | 1.372 ms | 0.552 ms | 2.49x |\n| 10,000, D=8, K=16 | 9.520 ms | 4.246 ms | 2.24x |\n| 10,000 separate, D=4, K=32 | 5.109 ms | 4.170 ms | 1.23x |\n\nReal device breakdown:\n\n| Phase | p50 | p95 |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| One-time workspace reserve | 45.245 ms | 46.958 ms |\n| Cold device call | 401.182 ms | 401.985 ms |\n| Warm device GPU | 356.277 ms | 358.379 ms |\n| Warm synchronized host | 356.552 ms | 358.439 ms |\n| 9.28M-edge device-to-host copy | 7.169 ms | 7.253 ms |\n\nThe brute-force crossover measurements support the dispatch rule:\n\n- 128/D=16/K=1: brute force 0.089 ms, grid 0.185 ms;\n- 128/D=3/K=4: brute force 0.055 ms, grid 0.167 ms;\n- 512/D=3/K=32 dense: grid 0.835 ms, brute force 1.121 ms;\n- 1,000/D=4/K=8 sparse: grid 0.217 ms, brute force 0.277 ms at the time of\n  the dispatch experiment.\n\nThe later 3D-grid/cell-cap optimization reduces the automatic 1,000/D=4/K=8\ncase further to 0.125 ms.\n\n## Memory, compilation, and regressions\n\nFor the real host call, major device allocations are approximately:\n\n| Storage | Size |\n|---|---:|\n| Neighbor distance/index workspace | 2,173,304,000 bytes |\n| Exact edge output | 148,474,752 bytes |\n| Input and sorted coordinates | 26,079,648 bytes |\n| Grid counts/offsets | 16,777,216 bytes |\n| Point cell metadata | 2,173,304 bytes |\n| Edge count/offset metadata | 4,346,624 bytes |\n| Approximate total before scan scratch | 2,261 MiB |\n\nCount-first host allocation avoids the 4,146 MiB conservative edge output\nthat would otherwise be required for N×K capacity.\n\nThe Release shared library grows from 168 KiB to 399 KiB (+231 KiB, 2.38x).\nA clean parallel build on this machine grows from 4.01 seconds to 8.40 seconds\n(2.09x), mainly from dimension-specialized kernels and the exact heap path.\n\nNo immutable primary benchmark regresses by 10%. Relative to the first broad\npost-optimization matrix, the D=8 duplicate distribution rises from 2.097 ms\nto 2.364 ms (+12.7%) while the rest of that matrix generally improves. This\nedge-case regression is retained because the final structural changes produce\nlarge gains on empty, boundary, clustered, dense-K, large D=8, and 500k-point\ncases, and the duplicate case remains exact.\n\n## Retained and rejected experiments\n\nRetained:\n\n- exact CUDA brute-force fallback and documented dispatch;\n- early distance exit and common-dimension specializations;\n- spatially ordered identical-set queries;\n- lazy neighbor sentinel and 32-bit internal indices;\n- dimension-sensitive 3D/4D grids and metadata ceilings;\n- exact count-first host output allocation;\n- density-triggered hybrid top-K heap.\n\nRejected or narrowed:\n\n- unconditional brute force for 512/D=3/K=32 and 1,000/D=4/K=8;\n- a universal 131,072-cell grid cap, which made the real D=12 case 2.25x\n  slower;\n- radius-sized cells for four grid dimensions, which regressed large D=8;\n- an unconditional K=64 heap, which doubled a sparse 50k-point case;\n- a fourth grid dimension for D=4, which regressed the small D=4 case;\n- thread-local K arrays, avoided after resource inspection showed existing\n  D=8/D=16 stack and register pressure.\n\n## Remaining bottlenecks\n\n- High-dimensional search still indexes only four coordinates and can visit\n  many false candidates for correlated or anisotropic data.\n- Dense grid scans operate on a dimensionality-selected ceiling rather than a\n  compact occupied-cell structure.\n- Per-query selection is serial even after switching to a heap; a\n  warp-cooperative design may help dense cells.\n- Edge count, scan, write, and count copy remain separate stages.\n- The synchronous host API still allocates a fresh workspace per call.\n- CUDA Graph capture and a sorted-key/occupied-cell redesign remain credible\n  larger projects, but neither had enough measured evidence to retain here.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```bash\ncmake -S . -B build \\\n  -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \\\n  -DFRNN_BUILD_TESTS=ON \\\n  -DFRNN_BUILD_BENCHMARKS=ON\ncmake --build build --parallel\nctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure\n\n./build/frnn_benchmark \\\n  --full --warmup 10 --iterations 50 \\\n  --output benchmarks/results/reproduction.csv\n\n./build/frnn_benchmark \\\n  --embedding data/embedding_data.csv \\\n  --warmup 10 --iterations 30 \\\n  --output benchmarks/results/reproduction-real.csv\n\npython tests/compare_reference_edges.py --minimum-agreement 1.0\n```\n\nFor the optional Python validation:\n\n```bash\npython -m pip install .\npython tests/test_python.py\n```\n\nFor installed-library consumer validation:\n\n```bash\ncmake --install build --prefix \"$PWD/install\"\ncmake -S tests/consumer -B build-consumer \\\n  -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=\"$PWD/install\"\ncmake --build build-consumer --parallel\n```\n\n## Final validation results\n\nThe required Release configuration, build, and CTest command completed\nsuccessfully. The shared library installed, configured in\n`tests/consumer`, built, and ran with output `1 0`. A separate\n`BUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF` Release build passed CTest, installed, and its static\nconsumer also built and ran with output `1 0`.\n\n`ldd build/libfrnn.so` reports CUDA runtime and standard system libraries\nonly. Source/dependency inspection confirms no production Torch, ATen, c10,\nPython, or TBB linkage. The only Python discovery in CMake is under the\ndisabled-by-default `FRNN_BUILD_PYTHON` option.\n\n## Suggested pull request\n\nTitle:\n\n```text\nOptimize exact standalone FRNN search and edge construction\n```\n\nDescription:\n\n```text\nAdds an exact CUDA brute-force fallback, dimension-sensitive 3D/4D grids,\nspecialized early-exit distance kernels, spatially ordered same-set queries,\nlazy compact neighbor rows, a density-triggered exact top-K heap, and\ncount-first host output allocation. Expands differential testing to all\ndispatch paths and adds reproducible synthetic/real benchmarks. The supplied\n271,663×12 dataset matches all 9,279,672 reference edges exactly. Representative\nwarm GPU p50 improves by 1.80x geometric mean, including 2.88x on small sparse\nand 2.55x on medium D=8 workloads.\n```\n","numLines":276,"startLine":1,"totalLines":276}}}
