{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user asked to continue optimizing the libFRNN CUDA library until the inference time for the real-life example (271,663 points × 12 dimensions, radius=0.12, K=1000, 9,279,672 directed edges) is below **200 ms** on the available GPU. The user wants continuous profiling, hypothesis-driven experiments, and benchmarking without changing correctness. The task was resumed from a previous conversation that had been cut off by context limits.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - **FRNN (Fixed-Radius Nearest Neighbors)**: Finding all neighbors within a radius, returning top-K by (distance, index) ordering\n   - **4D uniform grid spatial index**: Grid with 625-cell stencil (5^4), cell_size = radius/2\n   - **Real workload**: 271,663 × 12 dims, radius=0.12, K=1000, ~34 neighbors/query, ~871 candidates/query\n   - **Append-only + insertion sort**: Current neighbor collection approach (no sorted insertion during scan)\n   - **Float4 vectorization**: `*reinterpret_cast<const float4*>()` for 128-bit database loads, reducing serial dependency chains from 12 to 3\n   - **Warp divergence**: Early-exit branches causing threads in a warp to diverge; reducing from 12 to 3 branch points per candidate via float4 grouping\n   - **Register pressure**: D=12 kernel uses 62 registers (post-float4), down from 51 pre-float4, limiting occupancy to 33 warps/SM (51.6%)\n   - **SASS inspection**: Using `cuobjdump --dump-resource-usage` and `--dump-sass` to verify compiled code\n   - **LDG.E.CONSTANT.SYS**: 32-bit read-only cache loads generated by the compiler (float4 is decomposed to 4 separate 32-bit LDGs, not LDG.128)\n   - **GPU**: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2070 SUPER, SM 7.5, 40 SMs, 8 GiB, CUDA 12.0.140, built with -DCMAKE_CUDA_ARCHITECTURES=75\n   - **Occupancy**: 40→33 warps/SM after float4 change (62 regs × 33 = 2046 threads ≤ 65536 regs)\n   - **Heap functions**: `buildNeighborHeap`, `insertNeighborHeap`, `sortNeighborHeap` are dead code for K=1000 (found≈34 << K=1000) but still compiled/inlined\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - **`/workspace/libFRNN/src/frnn.cu`** — Main implementation file; all optimizations applied here.\n     - **Generic squaredDistance template** (unchanged):\n       ```cpp\n       template <int StaticDimension>\n       __device__ float squaredDistance(const float* lhs, const float* rhs,\n                                        int runtime_dimension, float limit) {\n         const int dimension = StaticDimension == 0 ? runtime_dimension : StaticDimension;\n         float distance = 0.0F;\n       #pragma unroll\n         for (int axis = 0; axis < dimension; ++axis) {\n           const float difference = lhs[axis] - rhs[axis];\n           distance += difference * difference;\n           if (distance > limit) break;\n         }\n         return distance;\n       }\n       ```\n     - **D=12 squaredDistance specialization** (current state, after all experiments):\n       ```cpp\n       // D=12: float4 loads the database candidate (LDG.128 = single 128-bit\n       // transaction per group) while reading the query from scalar registers.\n       template <>\n       __device__ float squaredDistance<12>(const float* __restrict__ lhs,\n                                            const float* __restrict__ rhs,\n                                            int /*runtime_dimension*/, float limit) {\n         const float4 l0 = *reinterpret_cast<const float4*>(lhs);\n         float t;\n         t = l0.x - rhs[0]; float dist = t * t;\n         t = l0.y - rhs[1]; dist += t * t;\n         t = l0.z - rhs[2]; dist += t * t;\n         t = l0.w - rhs[3]; dist += t * t;\n         if (dist > limit) return dist;\n         const float4 l1 = *reinterpret_cast<const float4*>(lhs + 4);\n         t = l1.x - rhs[4]; dist += t * t;\n         t = l1.y - rhs[5]; dist += t * t;\n         t = l1.z - rhs[6]; dist += t * t;\n         t = l1.w - rhs[7]; dist += t * t;\n         if (dist > limit) return dist;\n         const float4 l2 = *reinterpret_cast<const float4*>(lhs + 8);\n         t = l2.x - rhs[8]; dist += t * t;\n         t = l2.y - rhs[9]; dist += t * t;\n         t = l2.z - rhs[10]; dist += t * t;\n         t = l2.w - rhs[11]; dist += t * t;\n         return dist;\n       }\n       ```\n     - **findNeighborsBruteForce kernel signature** (with `__restrict__` and `__align__(16)`):\n       ```cpp\n       template <int StaticDimension>\n       __global__ void findNeighborsBruteForce(\n           const float* __restrict__ query, int query_count,\n           const float* __restrict__ database, int database_count, int dimension,\n           float radius, int max_neighbors, bool exclude_self, bool inputs_are_same,\n           float* neighbor_distances, int* neighbor_indices) {\n         // ...\n         __align__(16) float cached_query[StaticDimension == 0 ? 1 : StaticDimension];\n         // ...\n       }\n       ```\n     - **findNeighbors kernel signature** (with `__restrict__` and `__align__(16)`):\n       ```cpp\n       template <int StaticDimension>\n       __global__ void findNeighbors(\n           const float* __restrict__ query, int query_count,\n           const float* __restrict__ sorted_database,\n           const int* __restrict__ sorted_database_indices, int database_count,\n           int dimension, const GridParameters* __restrict__ parameters,\n           const int* __restrict__ grid_offsets, float radius, int max_neighbors,\n           bool exclude_self, bool inputs_are_same,\n           const int* __restrict__ query_indices, float* neighbor_distances,\n           int* neighbor_indices) {\n         // ...\n         __align__(16) float cached_query[StaticDimension == 0 ? 1 : StaticDimension];\n         // ...\n       }\n       ```\n     - **Heap functions** (currently back to normal inline `__device__`, after reverting `__noinline__`):\n       ```cpp\n       __device__ void insertNeighborHeap(...) { ... }\n       __device__ void sortNeighborHeap(...) { ... }\n       __device__ void buildNeighborHeap(...) { ... }\n       ```\n     - **Inner append loop** (no heap triggered for K=1000, found≈34):\n       ```cpp\n       if (!heap_mode && found < max_neighbors) {\n         distances[found] = distance;\n         indices[found] = database_index;\n         ++found;\n       } else {\n         if (!heap_mode) {\n           buildNeighborHeap(distances, indices, found);\n           heap_mode = true;\n         }\n         insertNeighborHeap(distance, database_index, max_neighbors, found, distances, indices);\n       }\n       ```\n     - **Post-loop sort**:\n       ```cpp\n       if (heap_mode) {\n         sortNeighborHeap(distances, indices, found);\n       } else {\n         for (int i = 1; i < found; ++i) {\n           const float d = distances[i];\n           const int idx = indices[i];\n           int j = i - 1;\n           while (j >= 0 && precedes(d, idx, distances[j], indices[j])) {\n             distances[j + 1] = distances[j];\n             indices[j + 1] = indices[j];\n             --j;\n           }\n           distances[j + 1] = d;\n           indices[j + 1] = idx;\n         }\n       }\n       if (found < max_neighbors) {\n         indices[found] = -1;\n       }\n       ```\n   - **`/workspace/libFRNN/docs/optimization_log.md`** — Experiment history (read for context)\n   - **`/workspace/libFRNN/docs/performance_report.md`** — Performance state (read for context)\n   - **`/workspace/libFRNN/docs/optimize_compute.md`** — Optimization goals and requirements (read for context)\n   - **`/workspace/libFRNN/benchmarks/frnn_benchmark.cu`** — Benchmark executable (pre-existing)\n\n4. Errors and Fixes:\n   - **`__noinline__` on heap functions increased register count from 62 to 80 with STACK:96**:\n     - Attempted: Adding `__device__ __noinline__` to `buildNeighborHeap`, `insertNeighborHeap`, `sortNeighborHeap`\n     - Result: Register count jumped from 62 to 80 and STACK went from 0 to 96 bytes (function call overhead requires saving/restoring more registers)\n     - Fix: Reverted all three functions to plain `__device__` (without `__noinline__`)\n   - **No-early-exit float4 was SLOWER (268 ms vs 253 ms)**:\n     - Attempted: All 3 float4 loads issued simultaneously, no branch points between groups\n     - Result: 268 ms — worse than 3-group early exit (253 ms)\n     - Conclusion: Early exit IS saving real work (reduced bandwidth from ~18 bytes/candidate avg vs 48 bytes/candidate)\n     - Fix: Reverted to 3-group float4 with early exit after each group\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - **Float4 gives 13.5% speedup (292.5→253.0 ms)**: The optimization reduces serial dependency chains in the distance computation from 12 per-dimension branch points to 3 per-group branch points. Even though the compiler decomposes float4 to 4 separate 32-bit LDGs (not actual LDG.128), the early-exit granularity change reduces warp divergence.\n   - **Real workload grid analysis**: Python script confirmed grid has 26×21×17×21 = 194,922 cells, stencil = 625 cells, 1.39 points/cell, 871 candidates/query average. All 625 stencil cells must be visited (boundary cells at ±2 cell offsets have minimum distance exactly equal to radius²).\n   - **Register bottleneck**: 62 registers limits occupancy to 33 warps/SM (51.6%). Original was 51 regs → 40 warps/SM (62.5%). Attempts to reduce registers have failed or made things worse.\n   - **Ongoing challenge**: Need additional 21% speedup (253→200 ms). Main remaining ideas:\n     - Template-based UseHeap=false kernel for large K (eliminates dead heap register allocation)\n     - Shared memory optimizations\n     - Texture memory for sorted_database\n     - Other structural changes\n\n6. All User Messages:\n   - \"Please read docs/optimize_compute.md for the 'goal' and docs/performance_report.md for the current performance report from Codex. Optimization logs can be found at docs/optimization_log.md. Your task is to continue the optimization by exploring different algorithmic designs and CUDA-specific improvements. You should not stop until the inference time for the real-life example using the available GPU is below 200 ms.\"\n   - \"Output token limit hit. Resume directly — no apology, no recap of what you were doing. Pick up mid-thought if that is where the cut happened. Break remaining work into smaller pieces.\"\n   - \"Output token limit hit. Resume directly — no apology, no recap of what you were doing. Pick up mid-thought if that is where the cut happened. Break remaining work into smaller pieces.\"\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - **PRIMARY**: Get warm GPU p50 inference time for real-life example (271,663 × 12, radius=0.12, K=1000) below **200 ms**. Currently at **253 ms**.\n   - Run correctness validation after any retained change: `python tests/compare_reference_edges.py --minimum-agreement 1.0`\n   - Update `docs/optimization_log.md` with experiment results\n   - Continue profiling and experimenting until < 200 ms achieved\n\n8. Current Work:\n   The most recent work was a failed attempt to reduce register count by adding `__noinline__` to heap functions (`buildNeighborHeap`, `insertNeighborHeap`, `sortNeighborHeap`). This made things worse (80 regs, STACK:96) so was reverted. The three functions are now back to plain `__device__` inline functions.\n\n   Current benchmark results (warm GPU p50):\n   - `device_warm_gpu`: **252.996 ms** (after float4 optimization)\n   - `stage_neighbor_search`: **249.596 ms**\n   - All other stages: < 4 ms combined\n\n   Register count for findNeighbors<12>: **62 registers** (up from 51 before float4, limiting occupancy to 33 warps/SM).\n\n   The SASS analysis revealed:\n   - Float4 is decomposed to 4 × LDG.E.CONSTANT.SYS (32-bit) per group, NOT LDG.128\n   - The compiler IS issuing multiple loads together before comparisons (good ILP)\n   - The 13.5% speedup comes from reducing early-exit branch points from 12 to 3 (less warp divergence)\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   The next optimization to try is implementing a `UseHeap` template parameter on the `findNeighbors` kernel to eliminate dead heap code for large K (like K=1000). This would reduce register pressure and potentially restore occupancy closer to 40 warps/SM.\n\n   Based on the analysis: for K=1000 with found≈34 neighbors, the heap code (`buildNeighborHeap`, `insertNeighborHeap`, `sortNeighborHeap`) is NEVER called, but the compiler still allocates registers for it, contributing to the 62-register count (vs 51 before). Compiling away the heap path via `if constexpr (!UseHeap)` in the kernel body could reduce register count and improve occupancy.\n\n   The safety concern: UseHeap=false is only correct when actual in-radius candidates < max_neighbors for all queries. For K=1000 with avg 34 neighbors, this should always hold. A heuristic dispatch based on `max_neighbors >= 128` (indicating a sparse search) would route K=1000 workloads to the no-heap version.\n\n   The current ongoing work is: \"continue the optimization by exploring different algorithmic designs and CUDA-specific improvements...until the inference time for the real-life example using the available GPU is below 200 ms.\"\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /root/.claude/projects/-workspace-libFRNN/6f82439e-67c9-4df8-8006-0f75deb44a8f.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6f82439e-67c9-4df8-8006-0f75deb44a8f","uuid":"c